using Microsoft.EntityFrameworkCore.Migrations;

#nullable disable

namespace HandyFix.Data.Migrations
{
    /// <inheritdoc />
    public partial class AddLocalTextToTenTownAreas : Migration
    {
        /// <inheritdoc />
        protected override void Up(MigrationBuilder migrationBuilder)
        {
            // More local text for the other ten of the first fifteen towns: Epsom, Sutton, Banstead,
            // Leatherhead, Wimbledon, Cobham, Walton-on-Thames & Weybridge, Reigate, Dorking and
            // Guildford (PROJECT_STATE Section 3eo). What each page said before stays, and the
            // town's facts follow in paragraphs, as for the five of AddLocalTextToFiveTownAreas. The
            // seeder fills an empty table only, so a database that already has the areas gets the text
            // here; the seeder holds the same text for a new database. By slug: on an empty table, or
            // for an area the admin has deleted, the UPDATE finds no row and does nothing.
            migrationBuilder.Sql(@"
UPDATE [ServiceAreas]
SET [IntroCopy] = N'Epsom has been one of our busiest areas for years, from the terraces near the town centre to the larger detached homes out towards the Downs and the racecourse.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'Epsom was a spa before it was a racing town. In the dry summer of 1618 cattle were seen to avoid one spring, the spring was made into a well, and the village grew quickly into a resort for Londoners. Epsom & Ewell Council''s appraisal of the town centre says many buildings of those years went up in haste, a timber frame faced in weatherboarding, and some still stand in South Street. The brickworks on the commons expanded from 1663 to meet the demand.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'The town as it is now took shape from the middle of the 19th century. Along College Road and the north-eastern end of Burgh Heath Road the later Victorian houses, some of them in terraces, are of local red brick with sash windows and panelled doorways under slate or tile. A panelled door of that age that sticks or has dropped on its hinges is a job we do by the hour.',
    [LocalNeighbourhoodsCopy] = N'We''re familiar with everything from the Victorian streets around Epsom station to the newer developments near Nonsuch Park and the period homes bordering Epsom Common.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'South of the station, the fields either side of Church Street were filled with houses and cottages between the 1870s and 1900, and the streets behind it, Heathcote Road and Laburnum Road among them, still have their 19th-century houses. Further out, the land along Burgh Heath Road became a spacious suburb between the late 1890s and the First World War. Lynwood Road has large detached houses with Arts and Crafts details, and the Chase Estate, which laid out Downs Avenue, also built in Temple Road, Chase Road and around West Hill.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'The Downs Road Estate is another thing altogether. Wates built it from the late 1950s to designs by their own architect, Kenneth Bland, with clay pantile roofs and, on some of the houses, a mansard first floor.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'By the Common, Stamford Green has late 19th-century cottages, many of them built to house staff of the five hospitals that went up nearby between 1890 and 1915. The first houses of the Hookfield estate were ready for tenants by 1921, and the housing round the Common went on improving through the 1920s and 30s.'
WHERE [Slug] = N'epsom';

UPDATE [ServiceAreas]
SET [IntroCopy] = N'Sutton''s mix of busy high-street flats and the leafier streets towards Cheam and Belmont keeps our technicians well practised across almost every job type.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'Stagecoaches made Sutton before trains did. From the 18th century it was the first stop on one of the most direct roads from London to Brighton. The railway came in 1847, with a second line in 1865 and a third in 1868, and Sutton Council''s character study dates the town''s building boom from there. Mains water and sewage works had arrived by 1863.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'Town and suburb meet without warning. The study notes how the tall buildings of the High Street give way at once to two-storey Victorian terraces, and south of the centre the blocks of flats built from the 1970s onwards stand where large Victorian and Edwardian houses used to be. If yours is one of the flats, tell us the floor and whether there is a lift when you book: it decides what we carry up.',
    [LocalNeighbourhoodsCopy] = N'From the Victorian terraces near Sutton station to the larger family homes around Rosehill and Benhilton, we''ve built up a good working knowledge of the area.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'East of the High Street, Newtown is two-storey Victorian terraces with small front gardens, and Victoria Road is much the same behind small walled ones. Round Beulah Road and Clarence Road the houses are late Victorian, with some early Edwardian semis. Landseer Road, to the south, is Edwardian and was finished by 1913.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'North of the centre most of the houses came in the early 20th century and between the wars: streets of semis, and Sutton Garden Suburb, begun in 1912 as cottage-style terraces and semis round small greens. The study says these houses have stayed much as they were built, apart from small extensions, loft conversions and front gardens turned into drives. Beyond them, at Rosehill, is the St Helier Estate, in its day one of the largest social housing estates in Europe.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'Belmont has grown bit by bit since its station opened in 1865. Carshalton changed faster: Carshalton Beeches station opened in 1906, and in the 1920s and 30s the fields round the old village and its ponds were covered with two-storey houses.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'The water is hard: 14 Clarke degrees in SES Water''s 2025 report for the town centre and Sutton North, and 15 in Carshalton and Wallington.'
WHERE [Slug] = N'sutton';

UPDATE [ServiceAreas]
SET [IntroCopy] = N'Banstead''s mix of large detached homes and village-edge cottages means jobs here are often bigger in scope, and our technicians carry the range of parts to match.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'For most of its history Banstead was a farming village on the chalk. In the 17th century it had a name as a health resort, with riding, hunting, shooting and horse racing, and in the 18th the big houses were built: Nork House, Garratts Hall, Banstead Place, Rooks Nest and Yewlands. What stands now is later. Reigate & Banstead Council''s design guide says the town centre reflects the building of the 1930s to the 1950s, with few buildings older than the 20th century.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'Those were the years when the farmland here, at Tadworth and at Burgh Heath went under housing estates: bay-windowed houses, almost all in brick and tile. The older material is flint. On the Downs it is in garden walls as much as in houses, usually framed in orange-red brick, so if a wall needs repair, tell us whether it is flint or brick when you book.',
    [LocalNeighbourhoodsCopy] = N'We know the leafy roads around Banstead Downs and the High Street, as well as the newer homes towards Tattenham Corner.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'The council''s guide picks out Longcroft Avenue as a highly decorated example of the houses of the 1930s to the 1950s, and its earlier edition names Nork among the suburbs of those years. Tadworth''s private estates were built for commuters, within a walk of the station.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'Kingswood grew up with its railway station too. The Kingswood Warren estate was sold in the early 20th century and built on over a number of years, as large houses at low density among the trees. The Preston Estate is council housing of the 1960s and 70s.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'The water is hard: 14 Clarke degrees in SES Water''s 2025 report for Nork and for Tattenham Corner. Further south, round Tadworth and Lower Kingswood, the figure is the same and SES classes it as moderately hard.'
WHERE [Slug] = N'banstead';

UPDATE [ServiceAreas]
SET [IntroCopy] = N'Leatherhead sits right on our route down the A243, so it''s an easy area for us to reach quickly, whatever the job.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'Leatherhead grew up where the River Mole could be forded, and was laid out round the crossroads of Bridge Street, High Street, North Street and Church Street, probably in the 13th century. It lived mostly by farming until the 20th. The railway came in 1859, and the land south of the High Street, old common-field strips crossed by Church Road, was built on only after 1870.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'So the old centre is small, and round it the houses are Victorian and Edwardian: brick and slate cottages on narrow streets and footpaths, and substantial red-brick villas on the steep hill of Park Rise. If your front door opens onto one of those footpaths, say so when you book, so we know how far the tools have to be carried.',
    [LocalNeighbourhoodsCopy] = N'Our technicians regularly work on the period homes around Leatherhead town centre, the riverside properties near the River Mole, and the family estates towards Fetcham and the Oxshott borders.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'Mole Valley Council''s design code for the town, published in November 2025, sorts the rest by age. Near the station most of the houses are from the 1930s, substantial and well built. North of the railway, older houses face Kingston Road and most of what lies behind is two large developments of the 1950s, semi-detached and terraced. Towards Epsom Road the houses and bungalows are mostly from the 1930s and the 1950s, in red brick, pale render and clay tile, with 1970s houses and flats at Levett Road and Cressall Mead. Along Reigate Road and Headley Road the houses are Edwardian and inter-war, each to its own design and set well back in its grounds.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'Flint is the local material beside the red brick. The retaining walls on Gimcrack Hill are flint, and very high.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'Fetcham grew most in the 1930s and the 1950s, when the estates in the north of the village were built. Its old centre is half a kilometre further south, on The Street, where the houses run from the early 17th century to the 20th.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'The water in Leatherhead, Fetcham and Bookham is moderately hard: 14 Clarke degrees in SES Water''s 2025 report.'
WHERE [Slug] = N'leatherhead';

UPDATE [ServiceAreas]
SET [IntroCopy] = N'Wimbledon''s grand Victorian houses and the busy flats around the Broadway and station keep our plumbing and carpentry teams equally busy.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'Wimbledon started at the top of the hill, where the Village is, and came down it. The railway opened in 1838 and the centre of the town moved to the station. In the second half of the 19th century villas and terraces spread out between the two, and Merton Council''s character study puts the peak of the building in the 1930s.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'The study also records what became of the biggest houses: during the Second World War many of the largest Victorian ones were divided into flats or replaced with blocks. If your home is a flat in one of them, tell us when you book how we get in: a street door of your own, an entry phone or a porter.',
    [LocalNeighbourhoodsCopy] = N'We''re well practised on the large period homes near Wimbledon Village and the Common, as well as the denser housing around South Wimbledon and Merton Park.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'East of the centre, round Queen''s Road, the streets are a grid of terraces, with double-fronted villas facing South Park Gardens. The terraces carry on north towards Durnsford Road and Plough Lane, dense and all of a piece, the Haydons Park estate among them. South-west of the centre, Wimbledon Chase is quiet Victorian and Edwardian streets, and the roads that climb west from Worple Road mix villas, terraced cottages and mansion blocks.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'Up the hill it opens out. On the Ridgway the plots are large and the ground steep, with detached houses, cottages and blocks of flats stepping up the slope. Parkside and West Wimbledon are substantial detached houses on big plots, many of them on quiet dead-end roads.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'Merton Park was developed by John Innes at the end of the 19th century, on early garden-suburb lines. The centre of South Wimbledon stands on what was Admiral Lord Nelson''s estate. Morden is later: the Northern line reached it in 1926, the streets grew up round the station, and most of it is suburban houses and cottage estates.'
WHERE [Slug] = N'wimbledon';

UPDATE [ServiceAreas]
SET [IntroCopy] = N'Cobham''s mix of period cottages around the village, larger family homes along Fairmile, and the newer executive developments near Stoke D''Abernon all come with their own maintenance quirks - from listed-building-sensitive repairs to keeping a large garden and outbuildings in order. Our technicians carry the range of tools and parts to handle both.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'Cobham used to be two places: Street Cobham on the Portsmouth Road and Church Cobham by the river, joined up mostly in the 20th century. The 19th century added streets to the north-east, and the railway, with stations at Stoke D''Abernon and Oxshott, brought more around 1900. After that came the private estates. Elmbridge Council''s guide describes how a large house would be pulled down and its grounds built over.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'Fairmile is a number of those estates, with hedges and trees for boundaries, no pavements and no street lighting. On a winter afternoon a house name is hard to read from the road, so give us a landmark when you book, or leave a light on.',
    [LocalNeighbourhoodsCopy] = N'Round Tartar Hill the streets are a tight late Victorian and Edwardian grid, terraces and semis with small front gardens, on the lower parts of Hogshill Lane, Cedar Road and Spencer Road. Freelands Road and the top of Hogshill Lane came next, with larger houses set back in their gardens.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'By the river, on Mill Road and The Tilt, the houses are older and individually designed, often in pairs and rarely over two storeys. Downside stands apart, across the Green Belt: cottage-sized houses round a large green, with post-war semis to the south. Near Stoke D''Abernon station the housing is denser, on narrow roads where most front gardens have become parking.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'Materials for higher-spec fixtures are sourced and agreed with you up front, so a premium finish never means a surprise on the invoice.'
WHERE [Slug] = N'cobham';

UPDATE [ServiceAreas]
SET [IntroCopy] = N'Walton-on-Thames and Weybridge''s riverside setting brings a steady mix of premium apartments and larger family homes, many changing hands or tenants often enough that a reliable local handyman matters.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'Walton is the largest town in Elmbridge, and most of it is 20th-century. Until the 1920s the south side of the High Street was the edge of Ashley Park, the grounds of a house built between 1602 and 1607. The house came down in 1929 and the park was sold off in half-acre plots from 1930. It is a private estate of large detached houses now.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'Weybridge began as a village at a ford on the River Wey and grew up beside two great estates, Oatlands Palace of the mid-16th century and Dorchester House of the 1670s. St George''s Hill came after: large gated houses on wooded plots that were, historically, never less than an acre.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'On plots of half an acre and more, the tap by the back door is a long way from the far end of the garden. A second outside tap is a small job, and one we fit.',
    [LocalNeighbourhoodsCopy] = N'We regularly work along the riverside developments near Walton Bridge, the period housing around Weybridge village, and the newer estates towards St George''s Hill.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'In Walton, Winchester Road was laid out in the late 19th century and became the spine of a tight grid of streets filled in over the next fifty years. East of Sidney Road the houses are inter-war and post-war, and Rydens has what Elmbridge Council''s guide calls possibly the largest concentration of single-storey and chalet-style houses in the borough. Old Walton, by the river, is modest two-storey houses where many of the roof spaces have been converted. The tallest flats are in the town centre, seven storeys at The Heart and ten beside Wellington Close.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'In Weybridge the roads off the High Street, to the north above all, are Victorian and Edwardian: red brick, sash windows and low front walls or hedges, with small gardens and parking on the street. Portmore Park Road has very late Victorian and Edwardian houses on large plots, and the villas and terraces round Heath Road, on the way in from the station, are of the same years.'
WHERE [Slug] = N'walton-on-thames-weybridge';

UPDATE [ServiceAreas]
SET [IntroCopy] = N'Reigate''s mix of period homes near the town centre and larger properties towards the hill keeps our technicians on their toes, and it''s an area we''re keen to build a stronger presence in.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'Reigate was laid out as a planned town before 1170, after the Warennes built the castle. The road to Brighton was turnpiked in 1755, the station opened in 1849, and by 1860 many houses had been built for people who travelled to work in London. In 1921 ""the Great Sale of Reigate"" put shops, hotels, businesses and homes on the market, and Reigate & Banstead Council''s design guide records that the rebuilding which followed cost the town many fine Georgian houses.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'Reigate has a stone of its own, dug at Merstham: greenish-grey and easily worked, which means it wears. The guide says it is rare on houses, where brick with hung tiles on the upper floor is the usual thing. Where an old wall or a step is Reigate stone, expect wear. Small repairs to brickwork and paving are our work; a stone wall we look at first, and say plainly whether it is a job for us or for a mason.',
    [LocalNeighbourhoodsCopy] = N'From the Georgian and Victorian houses around Reigate High Street to the family homes towards Redhill and Merstham, we cover the full spread of property types here.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'Redhill is the younger of the two towns. It was a hamlet in the early 19th century and grew with the London to Brighton railway and its station of 1841, housing railway workers as well as commuters. Much of its old centre went in the rebuilding of the 1960s to the 1980s.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'Council housing arrived between the wars, with later estates at Colesmead and Meadvale, and the big estates round Merstham were built for Londoners moving out after the war.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'SES Water''s 2025 report gives 13 Clarke degrees for the centre of Reigate and for Redhill, and 12 for South Park and Woodhatch. It classes both as moderately hard.'
WHERE [Slug] = N'reigate';

UPDATE [ServiceAreas]
SET [IntroCopy] = N'Dorking''s market-town character means a lot of older housing stock, and our technicians are experienced at working carefully around period features without slowing the job down.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'Dorking has had a market since at least 1278, when its lord claimed one by ancient right. The sandstone under the town is soft enough to dig, and there are caves cut into it behind the High Street and South Street, some of them made by mining the sand. What it was known for, though, was lime. Mole Valley Council''s appraisal of the town records that London bricklayers favoured Dorking lime for their mortar and that, because it set under water, it went into the building of the London docks in 1805.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'The town''s older walls were laid and plastered with lime, not cement. Tell us the age of your house when you book brickwork or plastering, so that the right materials come with us.',
    [LocalNeighbourhoodsCopy] = N'We cover the historic streets around Dorking High Street, the surrounding villages towards Box Hill, and the newer estates on the town''s edges.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'South of the centre, where the town spread in the 19th century either side of the Horsham Road, the streets are Victorian and each has its own look: the plain terraced cottages of Mount Street, with no front gardens, against the semi-detached villas of Howard Road. Rose Hill is a set piece, Italianate and Tudor villas of 1838 to 1860 round a paddock where livestock still graze.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'The 1930s took the town north-east, towards the meeting of the A24 and the A25. Along Deepdene Avenue and its side roads the houses are of the 1930s and the 1950s, and north of Ashcombe Road they are mainly detached and of the 1930s. Tower Hill, steep and looping, was largely built up in the 1970s.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'The estates are on the southern edge. The Chart Downs houses were built by the council in the 1950s, in red brick and plain clay tile round a green. Goodwyns has terraces, small blocks of flats and, rare for a small market town, two tower blocks. Between them they have joined North Holmwood, once a village two kilometres out, to the town. Pixham, across the railway, has a church by Lutyens of 1903.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'SES Water''s 2025 report puts Dorking''s water at 14 Clarke degrees, which it classes as moderately hard.'
WHERE [Slug] = N'dorking';

UPDATE [ServiceAreas]
SET [IntroCopy] = N'Guildford is the largest town in our Surrey coverage, from the historic streets around the castle and High Street to the newer estates on the outskirts - and we''re ready to take on the full range of handyman and plumbing work it brings.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'Guildford grew where the old road along the chalk ridge dropped down to cross the River Wey. The chalk is still part of the town: the assessment written for Guildford Borough Council notes disused quarries and the entrances to old mines of clunch, the chalk stone that Castle Street and the castle were built with. The Wey was made navigable in 1653, the railway came in 1845, and it was the railway that set the population growing.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'A good deal of the town is on a slope. If there are steps up to your door, say so when you book anything heavy to be fitted, a washing machine or a radiator, so we come ready for the carry.',
    [LocalNeighbourhoodsCopy] = N'We work across the period terraces near the town centre, the family homes towards Merrow and Burpham, and the newer developments around Park Barn.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'Charlotteville, south-east of the centre, was the first large estate. Dr T. Jenner Sells owned the land, named it after his wife and named the streets after famous doctors. Unusually, villas for the middle classes and smaller houses for workers were built side by side there.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'The streets nearest the centre are late Victorian: straight roads of two-storey terraces and semis, with larger villas, many of them now flats, in brick with a little stone or flint. There are late Victorian terraces along Station View and north of North Street, and Georgian villas on the Portsmouth Road.' + NCHAR(10) + NCHAR(10) + N'Onslow Village was begun after the First World War, when soldiers coming home made the shortage of houses worse, on the hillside of the old royal deer park west of the town. The Downs limit building to the south, so the council estates that followed went north. Bellfields and Park Barn are large post-war estates. Since the 1960s more have been added at Rydes Hill and Boxgrove, and out at Burpham and Merrow.'
WHERE [Slug] = N'guildford';
");
        }

